In this diagram, the first polygon is a regular pentagon, and the second is a regular triangle. What is the value, in degrees, of the sum of the measures of angles $ABC$ and $ABD$?

[asy]
draw(10dir(18)--10dir(90)--10dir(162)--10dir(234)--10dir(306)--cycle,linewidth(2));  // Pentagon
draw(10dir(234)--10dir(306)--10dir(306)+(0,-10)--10dir(234)+(0,-10)--10dir(234)--cycle,linewidth(2));  // Triangle

label("A",10dir(234),W);
label("B",10dir(306),E);
label("C",10dir(18),E);  // A point on the pentagon
label("D",10dir(306)+(0,-10),E);  // A point on the triangle

draw(10dir(306)+2dir(-135)--10dir(306)+dir(-135),linewidth(2));
[/asy]

A figure is constructed from unit cubes. Each cube shares at least one face with another cube. What is the minimum number of cubes needed to build a figure with the front and side views shown? [asy]
/* AMC8 2003 #15 Problem */
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(1,1)--(1,2)--(0,2)--cycle);
draw((0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0));
draw((4,0)--(6,0)--(6,2)--(5,2)--(5,1)--(4,1)--cycle);
draw((5,0)--(5,1)--(6,1));
label(scale(0.8)*"FRONT", (1, 0), S);
label(scale(0.8)*"SIDE", (5,0), S);
[/asy]

Let $P(x)$ be a polynomial of degree $3n$ such that
\begin{align*} P(0) = P(3) = \dots = P(3n) &= 2, \\ P(1) = P(4) = \dots = P(3n-2) &= 1, \\ P(2) = P(5) = \dots = P(3n-1) &= 0. \end{align*}
Also, $P(3n+1) = 730$. Determine $n$.

